            <ArticleTitle>Subtotal ostectomy of impinging dorsal spinous processes in 23 standing horses.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>95-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To (1) describe a safe, efficient surgical technique for subtotal ostectomy (SO) of diseased dorsal spinal processes (DSP) of the caudal thoracic vertebrae with the horse standing and (2) report outcome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case series.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="ANIMALS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Horses (n = 23) with reduced performance caused by impingement of the DSP of the caudal thoracic vertebrae.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Affected DSP were resected through a dorsal median incision with the horse standing, sedated, and the surgical site desensitized with local anesthetic. Radiography was used to confirm removal of impinging bone before wound closure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No serious complications occurred. Outcome was obtained for 22 horses. At &lt;1 year, 19 horses (86%) returned to full athletic function, 2 horses (9%) improved but had failed to return to full function, 1 horse had no improvement. At &gt;1 year, 2 horses that had returned to full athletic function were unable to function at full capacity; 1 was considered improved, but the other had not improved. Athletic function in 1 horse improved 6 months after surgery, but at 2.5 years was no better than before surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Subtotal ostectomy of impinging DSPs can be performed with good results with the horse standing.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
